<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Fe de Nuevo México</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1598">1598</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nuevo México</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa Fe de Nuevo México was a province on the northern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a>'s colonising expedition in 1598, with its capital at Santa Fe from about 1610. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> drove the Spanish out from 1680 to 1692. From 1776 it fell under the Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces; it became a territory of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1821, was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1846 and ceded in 1848.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means Holy Faith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>. It did not come from the independent nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>: as early as 1561 Spanish explorers used el Nuevo México for Cíbola, cities of wealth said to lie far north of the conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, and the Crónica Mexicayotl of 1609 identifies it with Aztlán, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> homeland. The province centred on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> valley and took in most of the present state of New Mexico with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> panhandle. Spanish settlement reached from Taos pueblo in the north to Albuquerque in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Coronado Expedition of 1540–1542 fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> in the Tiguex War before withdrawing. In 1598 the All Pueblo Council allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a>'s expedition of 500 men, which he financed himself, to settle the Rio Grande Valley, and on 12 July 1598 he founded the colony at San Juan de los Caballeros beside Ohkay Owingeh Pueblo. His rule was marked by great cruelty, and the king removed him; he was found guilty of cruelty, immorality and false reporting. His successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-peralta">Pedro de Peralta</a>, founded Santa Fe in 1610.</p><p>Franciscan missionaries baptised thousands, mostly Pueblo people, in the 1600s, while destroying religious objects, banning ceremonies and persecuting holy men. Twelve years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680, the Spanish returned at the invitation of some Pueblo elders, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> reoccupied Santa Fe. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> the 1824 constitution made it the federally administered Territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>. Antonio Armijo's expedition of 1829 opened a trade route west, and his scout Raphael Rivera found Las Vegas Springs. Mestizo and Pueblo inhabitants rose against the Centralist Republic in the Río Arriba Rebellion of 1837, and in 1841 New Mexican troops under Governor Manuel Armijo captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texan-santa-fe-expedition">Texan Santa Fe Expedition</a>. The U.S. Army under Stephen Kearny occupied the territory in 1846.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, the capital at Santa Fe and the governor's seat at the Palace of the Governors passed to the New Mexico Territory and to the state of New Mexico, admitted in 1912. Its Hispano and Pueblo inhabitants became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> citizens under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> peoples were denied citizenship until 1924.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santa Fe de Nuevo México” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war ended with the Mexican Cession, which added Alta California and <b>Nuevo México</b> to the United States.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/manifest-destiny">Manifest destiny · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The commandant general held military, political and judicial authority over Nueva Vizcaya, Sonora and Sinaloa, the Californias, <b>New Mexico</b>, Coahuila and Texas, and at times Nuevo León and Nuevo Santander.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>American armies took Mexico City in September 1847, and the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo of 1848 ceded California, <b>New Mexico</b> and the claim to Texas.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-federal-republic-of-mexico">Second Federal Republic of Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
